(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-male-gu-prostate-quiz"]={"title":"Male GU (genitourinary) & Prostate Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"During the male GU (genitourinary) health history, asking about painful or irregular erections with curvature specifically screens for...","choices":["epididymitis","benign prostatic hyperplasia","Peyronie's disease","testicular torsion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Painful or irregular erections with curvature is a classic history clue for Peyronie's disease, a condition involving fibrous plaque formation in the penile shaft that causes abnormal curvature during erection. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Lower urinary tract symptoms, one of the screened history items, would include complaints such as...","choices":["chest pain, shortness of breath, or exertional palpitations","urinary hesitancy, weak stream, frequency, or nocturia","blurred vision, visual field loss, or eye pain","joint pain, morning stiffness, and visible swelling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lower urinary tract symptoms encompass a cluster of voiding complaints including hesitancy, weak stream, urinary frequency, and nocturia, often prompting further evaluation for conditions like BPH (benign prostatic hyperplasia). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"If a patient develops an erection during the genital exam, the recommended examiner response is to...","choices":["calmly explain that it is a normal response, complete the exam, and maintain a composed demeanor","scold the patient sharply and visibly for having this entirely involuntary physiologic reaction","immediately stop the entire exam and leave the room abruptly without offering any explanation","document it as a definite abnormal finding that requires urgent specialist referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"An erection during the exam is a normal physiologic response; the examiner should calmly reassure the patient, complete the necessary examination, and maintain a professional, composed demeanor throughout. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"To help address patient apprehension before beginning the genital exam, the examiner should...","choices":["ask the patient to reschedule the entire visit if they appear nervous or hesitant","skip any explanation at all in order to save time during a busy clinic day","proceed immediately with the exam without ever discussing the process beforehand","thoroughly explain the examination process and, if needed, have an assistant present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Thoroughly explaining what the exam will involve helps reassure and inform the patient, and having an assistant (chaperone) present when needed provides additional comfort and appropriate practice safeguards. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"When the patient is supine for the genital exam, proper draping technique involves...","choices":["keeping the entire body fully covered at all times, including whichever specific area is being actively examined","using a gown to cover the chest/abdomen and placing a drape at the midthigh, exposing only the genitalia/inguinal area as needed","completely undressing the patient entirely, with no gown or drape used anywhere throughout the exam","draping and gowning are considered entirely unnecessary for this particular type of intimate genital exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Proper technique covers the chest and abdomen with a gown and places a drape at the midthigh, exposing only the genitalia and inguinal areas actually being examined at any given moment, preserving patient modesty. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"The standard sequence of the male genital physical exam includes inspecting the penis, urethral meatus, and scrotum, then...","choices":["auscultating the scrotum and inguinal region with a stethoscope before any palpation begins","palpating the shaft of the penis, assessing for groin hernias, and palpating the epididymis and each testis","only continuing visual inspection further, with no palpation ever performed at any point","immediately performing a DRE (digital rectal examination), skipping every other exam step entirely"],"answer":1,"exp":"After inspection, the exam proceeds to palpation: the penile shaft, assessment for groin hernias (inspection and palpation without and with Valsalva), and palpation of the epididymis and each testis, followed by examination of the spermatic cord. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Assessing for a groin hernia involves inspection and palpation performed...","choices":["only in the standing position, never supine","only with the patient lying flat and relaxed, never straining","only by auscultation","both without and with the Valsalva maneuver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hernia assessment specifically involves examining the groin both at rest and with the patient performing a Valsalva maneuver (bearing down), since increased intra-abdominal pressure can reveal a hernia bulge not otherwise apparent. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Transillumination, one of the special techniques for assessing scrotal contents, is most useful for distinguishing...","choices":["a fluid-filled mass (which transilluminates/glows) from a solid mass (which does not)","left-sided testicular size from right-sided testicular size differences alone","normal urine coloration from abnormal, cloudy discoloration seen in the urine","arterial blood flow from venous blood flow within the spermatic cord itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Transillumination involves shining a light source through the scrotal skin; a fluid-filled structure like a hydrocele will transilluminate (glow red/orange), while a solid mass (such as a tumor) will not, helping distinguish between these possibilities. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Regarding prostate cancer health promotion and counseling, which statement is accurate?","choices":["It is diagnosed with roughly equal frequency in men younger than forty and men considerably older","It is one of the most frequently diagnosed cancers in US men, rare before age 40, with higher incidence in African American men","It has a distinctly lower incidence in African American men than in other racial groups nationally","It is one of the least frequently diagnosed cancers among men anywhere in the United States today"],"answer":1,"exp":"Prostate cancer is among the most frequently diagnosed cancers in US men; it is rare before age 40, and epidemiologic data show a higher incidence in African American men, all relevant points for health promotion/counseling discussions. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is referenced as a tool for...","choices":["screening for prostate cancer","assessing kidney function","screening for testicular cancer","diagnosing erectile dysfunction"],"answer":0,"exp":"PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is specifically referenced as a screening test used in the context of prostate cancer health promotion and counseling. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Performing a testicular self-examination, one of the special techniques taught to patients, is best done...","choices":["monthly, ideally after a warm shower when the scrotal skin is relaxed","only during a cold shower, when the scrotal skin is taut","only a single time, during the patient's very first urology visit","only by a clinician annually, never performed by the patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Testicular self-exam is typically recommended on a monthly basis, ideally after a warm shower when the scrotal skin is relaxed, making it easier to detect any new masses or changes. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Examining the spermatic cord, part of the standard male GU (genitourinary) exam sequence, is performed by...","choices":["palpating along its length from the testis up to the external inguinal ring","measuring blood pressure directly over the femoral pulse in the groin","percussing gently over the scrotal skin with a plexor finger","auscultating for bowel sounds transmitted down into the groin region"],"answer":0,"exp":"The spermatic cord is examined by palpating along its length, from the testis up toward the external inguinal ring, assessing for masses, tenderness, or thickening (which could suggest a varicocele or other pathology). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 'bag of worms' texture palpated above the testis, classically more common on the left side, is the description associated with...","choices":["testicular torsion","a varicocele","a hydrocele","a normal testis"],"answer":1,"exp":"A varicocele — dilated, tortuous veins of the pampiniform plexus — classically produces a 'bag of worms' texture on palpation above the testis, and is more common on the left side due to venous drainage anatomy (left testicular vein draining into the left renal vein at a less favorable angle). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"A hydrocele is best described as...","choices":["a painless, fluid-filled swelling of the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis","a normal anatomic finding present in essentially every male patient","a description of dilated, tortuous veins within the spermatic cord","a solid, tender mass that arises directly from the testis itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"A hydrocele is a painless collection of fluid within the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis, producing a smooth scrotal swelling that classically transilluminates. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Acute, severe scrotal pain accompanied by a high-riding, transversely-oriented testis and absent cremasteric reflex is most concerning for...","choices":["a routine finding needing no urgency at all","testicular torsion, a surgical emergency","a normal variant seen in some healthy men","Peyronie's disease, a penile curvature condition"],"answer":1,"exp":"This presentation is classic for testicular torsion — twisting of the spermatic cord cutting off blood supply — which is a surgical emergency requiring immediate evaluation to preserve testicular viability. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Palpating each testis during the exam, the examiner should assess for...","choices":["size, shape, consistency, and any masses or tenderness","chest wall expansion and respiratory symmetry","blood pressure and resting pulse rate","bowel sounds heard in all four abdominal quadrants"],"answer":0,"exp":"Systematic testicular palpation assesses size, shape, consistency (should be smooth and firm, not nodular or stony), and screens for any masses or areas of tenderness. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Asking about penile discharge or lesions as part of the GU (genitourinary) history can help identify conditions such as...","choices":["sexually transmitted infections","hearing loss","only prostate cancer","only benign prostatic hyperplasia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Penile discharge or lesions are classic presenting symptoms of several sexually transmitted infections, making this a key screening question in the male GU (genitourinary) history. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Wearing gloves during the male GU (genitourinary) exam is a standard requirement primarily because...","choices":["it is entirely optional per standard technique, left up to the examiner's personal preference","it eliminates the entirely separate need to explain the exam process to the patient beforehand","it meaningfully improves the examiner's fine tactile sensitivity during genital palpation exams","it protects both patient and examiner per standard precautions when examining genitalia/mucous membranes"],"answer":3,"exp":"As with any exam involving contact with genitalia or potential mucosal/body fluid exposure, gloves are required under standard precautions to protect both the patient and the examiner. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"An indirect inguinal hernia differs from a direct inguinal hernia primarily in its anatomic path, in that an indirect hernia...","choices":["passes through the internal inguinal ring, following the path of the spermatic cord, and can descend into the scrotum","always occurs exclusively in female patients, since the spermatic cord pathway doesn't exist in men at all","never reaches the scrotum, remaining confined entirely above the level of the inguinal ligament always","bulges directly through Hesselbach's triangle, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, every single time"],"answer":0,"exp":"An indirect inguinal hernia protrudes through the internal inguinal ring and follows the same path as the spermatic cord through the inguinal canal, and can extend into the scrotum, whereas a direct hernia bulges through a weakness in the abdominal wall (Hesselbach's triangle) medial to the inferior epigastric vessels. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Inspecting the urethral meatus as part of the genital exam allows the examiner to check for...","choices":["visual acuity and near/far pupillary accommodation reflex testing bilaterally","lung sounds heard bilaterally during a routine chest auscultation exam","discharge, lesions, or abnormal positioning of the opening (for example, hypospadias)","heart rate, rhythm regularity, and point of maximal cardiac impulse location"],"answer":2,"exp":"Inspecting the urethral meatus screens for discharge (which may suggest infection), lesions, and abnormal positioning of the opening such as hypospadias (urethral opening on the ventral penile shaft rather than the tip). — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Reviewing a younger patient's sexual maturity rating during the GU (genitourinary) exam serves mainly to...","choices":["assess entirely unrelated cardiac function through auscultation of heart sounds directly","determine the patient's blood type through a rapid bedside antigen agglutination test","accurately document pubertal developmental stage and distinguish normal variation from abnormal findings","entirely replace the separate need for performing the rest of the physical examination"],"answer":2,"exp":"Documenting sexual maturity rating provides a standardized way to record a younger patient's pubertal developmental stage, helping distinguish normal developmental variation from a genuinely abnormal finding. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"A firm, non-tender nodule palpated on the testis itself (not the epididymis) should raise concern for...","choices":["benign prostatic hyperplasia, a prostate condition","testicular cancer, warranting further urgent workup","a normal finding requiring no further workup","a normal variant seen commonly in most healthy men"],"answer":1,"exp":"A firm, painless nodule on the testis itself is a classic concerning finding for testicular cancer and should prompt urgent further evaluation (for example, ultrasound), unlike epididymal findings, which are more often benign/infectious/cystic in nature. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"Scrotal or testicular pain, swelling, or lesions, as a screened history item, is important to ask about because it can indicate conditions ranging from...","choices":["exclusively cardiac conditions, since scrotal vessels are said to share the heart's blood supply","only minor, entirely self-limited issues that never require any urgent clinical attention","benign cysts to urgent conditions like torsion, requiring careful triage based on onset and severity","exclusively dermatologic conditions, confined strictly to the surface of the scrotal skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Scrotal/testicular symptoms span a wide range of causes — from relatively benign findings like epididymal cysts to time-sensitive emergencies like testicular torsion — making careful history-taking about onset, severity, and associated symptoms essential for appropriate triage. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Because prostate cancer is often asymptomatic in its early stages, the health promotion emphasis for this condition centers on...","choices":["treating every single patient completely identically, regardless of their individual age or family risk factors","appropriate screening discussions (for example, PSA (prostate-specific antigen) testing) rather than waiting for symptoms to appear","relying solely on patient self-examination performed at home, exactly as taught for testicular self-exams","simply ignoring the condition entirely until noticeable symptoms eventually happen to clearly develop"],"answer":1,"exp":"Since early prostate cancer often produces no symptoms, health promotion focuses on informed screening discussions (such as PSA (prostate-specific antigen) testing) tailored to patient risk factors, rather than waiting for symptomatic presentation. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"A spermatocele, a possible finding on scrotal exam, is best described as...","choices":["a solid, non-cystic mass that arises directly from the substance of the testis itself and grows","a painless, sperm-containing cystic mass typically located above/behind the testis, near the epididymis","identical to a varicocele in both its diffuse texture and its typical location above the testis","always a definitive sign of testicular malignancy requiring immediate surgical intervention"],"answer":1,"exp":"A spermatocele is a painless, fluid/sperm-filled cystic mass usually found above or behind the testis in association with the epididymis, generally a benign finding distinct from the testis itself. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Having an assistant/chaperone present during the male genital exam, when appropriate, primarily serves to...","choices":["is legally required for every single patient, regardless of their stated personal preference","provide additional patient comfort and support appropriate examination practice standards","is never considered appropriate at any point during this particular type of intimate exam","entirely replaces the examiner's separate need to fully explain the exam process beforehand"],"answer":1,"exp":"A chaperone can help the patient feel more comfortable during a sensitive exam and supports appropriate practice standards for conducting intimate examinations professionally. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 5"},{"q":"Inspecting the scrotum as part of the exam includes assessing its...","choices":["size, symmetry, and any visible skin changes or masses","near and distance visual acuity testing bilaterally","resting heart rate and pulse rhythm regularity","lung sounds heard bilaterally on chest auscultation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Scrotal inspection assesses overall size, symmetry between the two sides, and any visible skin changes (rashes, lesions) or masses, providing the initial visual screen before palpation. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A thorough male GU (genitourinary) history establishes context that helps the examiner...","choices":["skip the physical exam entirely whenever the patient's history seems reassuring or unremarkable","avoid the separate legal requirement of obtaining patient consent to the examination itself","focus the subsequent physical exam on the most relevant findings and differential considerations","is entirely irrelevant to any of the subsequent physical exam findings that are gathered"],"answer":2,"exp":"A focused history (urinary symptoms, discharge, pain, sexual function) helps the examiner anticipate what to look for on exam and narrows the relevant differential before physical examination even begins. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"An enlarged, tender prostate on DRE (digital rectal examination), especially with systemic symptoms like fever, is most consistent with...","choices":["testicular torsion","acute prostatitis","a normal prostate exam finding","a hydrocele"],"answer":1,"exp":"An acutely tender, often boggy/enlarged prostate accompanied by systemic symptoms like fever is classic for acute prostatitis, an infectious/inflammatory process, in contrast to the typically non-tender enlargement of BPH (benign prostatic hyperplasia) or the hard nodularity of malignancy. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"Because prostate cancer risk and screening recommendations can vary by individual factors, a thorough approach to counseling should account for...","choices":["only the patient's age, deliberately ignoring both race and family history factors","a strictly identical screening recommendation given to every single patient seen","the patient's age, race, and family history alongside a discussion of screening benefits/risks","ignoring family history entirely, while weighing only the patient's age and race"],"answer":2,"exp":"Individualized counseling considers age, race (for example, higher incidence in African American men), and family history alongside a balanced discussion of the benefits and limitations of screening tools like PSA (prostate-specific antigen), rather than a one-size-fits-all recommendation. — Male GU (genitourinary) and Prostate Examination lecture, Slide 10"}]};
